      meta: 'Published on March 1, 2026 · 6 min read',
      body: `<p class="intro-lead">Talking about AI automations is no longer talking about the future. Many businesses are already using them in customer service, document handling, internal analysis and operations. The relevant question now is not whether it works, but where it creates the most impact.</p><h2>Examples of companies already applying it</h2><p>Common cases include businesses that automate first-line support, invoice extraction, lead follow-up and internal reporting. The use case changes, but the pattern is similar: repetitive work gets handled faster and with more consistency.</p><h2>Results that repeat across most cases</h2><ul><li>Hours saved in operational work</li><li>Fewer manual mistakes</li><li>Faster response times</li><li>Better visibility of information</li></ul><h2>Why these companies are seeing ROI</h2><p>Because they usually start with a specific bottleneck instead of trying to automate everything at once. That focus makes the benefit measurable.</p><h2>What other companies can learn</h2><p>The right way to adopt AI is not to ask for “an AI project” in the abstract, but to identify a process that is repetitive, costly and clearly improvable.</p><div class="cta-section"><p>Want to assess which case in your company could deliver a similar return?</p><a href="https://jjodevelopers.cloud/" class="btn-cta">Visit JJO</a></div>`
