      title: '7 business processes that make the most sense to automate with AI',
      meta: 'Published on March 2, 2026 · 6 min read',
      body: `<p class="intro-lead">Not every business process should be automated first. When AI is introduced, the difference between getting quick ROI and getting frustrated often comes down to choosing the right starting point.</p><h2>Processes that usually work best</h2><ul><li>Email and request classification</li><li>Initial replies to clients and leads</li><li>Document reading and data extraction</li><li>Commercial follow-up</li><li>Internal reporting</li><li>Knowledge bases and internal support</li><li>System and record updates</li></ul><h2>What these processes have in common</h2><p>They tend to be frequent, repetitive, rules-based and time-consuming. That makes them easier to automate safely and easier to measure afterwards.</p><div class="highlight-box"><p><strong>The smartest first automation is usually not the flashiest one.</strong> It is the one that removes recurring friction and gives the team time back.</p></div><p>At <a href="https://jjodevelopers.cloud/" style="color:#60a5fa;">JJO</a> we help companies choose where to start so automation creates value quickly instead of becoming an abstract initiative.</p><div class="cta-section"><p>Want to identify which of these processes exists in your company today?</p><a href="https://jjodevelopers.cloud/" class="btn-cta">Visit JJO</a></div>`
